course: dictionary
lesson: 4908
title: Imagination
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Imagination is the ability to make pictures, ideas and stories in your mind, about things that are not real or not here right now. Writers, artists and players all use it. When a teacher says «use your imagination», she wants you to think up new ideas.
explain: «Paintings of history often mix fact, legend and the painter's imagination.» The words "fact" and "legend" show what the painter mixes in: the true facts and the made-up stories. So imagination here means the painter's own invented pictures in the mind, not the real events.
explain: Imagination can also be something only in your mind. If someone says «It's just your imagination», the words "just" and "your" are the clues. They mean the fear or idea is not real, only in your head.
fact: Imagination is the ability to make pictures, ideas and stories in your mind.
fact: «Imagination» can mean clever new ideas, like the ideas scientists think up to test.
fact: «A vivid imagination» describes a lively, clear imagination; on a school report it can also mean the pupil tells fibs or daydreams.
fact: «Use your imagination» is what teachers say when they want you to invent ideas.
fact: Young children often mix up wishing, pretending and what really happened, so their tall tales come from imagination, not wicked lies.
exercise: «Paintings of history often mix fact, legend and the painter's imagination.» What does imagination mean here? | A kind of paint | *Invented pictures and ideas the painter adds in the mind | A museum ticket | Exact true facts from books
exercise: Ada Lovelace mixed imagination and numbers into what she called «poetical science». What did she mix with numbers? | *Imagination | Silence | Sleep | Sandwiches
exercise: «It's just your imagination», said Mum when I heard a noise. What does this tell you? | Mum is very angry | *The fear is not real, only in my head | The room is very big | The noise was a dragon
test: «In The Lion King musical, your imagination does the rest, like when you play "this sofa is a pirate ship".» What does "your imagination does the rest" suggest? | *You fill in what you cannot see with ideas in your mind | You must leave the theatre | Only the animals are real | The sofa really becomes a ship
test: Teacher says «Use your imagination». What is she asking you to do? | Stop thinking and sit still | *Think up new ideas and pictures in your mind | Copy the book exactly | Go home early
exam: On a report, «vivid imagination» can mean the pupil | has painted a very good picture | can run very fast | *tells fibs or daydreams | has lost a sock
